  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On 2026-06-12, Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif stated that a &ldquo;final, agreed upon text&rdquo; of a US-Iran peace agreement had been reached through Pakistan&rsquo;s mediation. Iranian FM Araghchi followed with a statement on 2026-06-13 characterizing the Islamabad MOU as &ldquo;closer to finalisation than ever before&rdquo; and describing a two-stage structure in which the nuclear file is deferred to a second phase. Trump stated a signing ceremony could occur &ldquo;this weekend in Europe&rdquo; with VP Vance attending. No confirmed face-to-face session between Iranian and US delegations has been documented for June&nbsp;10-13. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">NF1</span></p>
      <p>Iran&rsquo;s domestic diplomatic posture remained divided across institutional lines through June&nbsp;11-13. Foreign Ministry spokesperson Baghaei (IRNA) stated on June&nbsp;11 that reports of a finalized agreement were &ldquo;merely speculation,&rdquo; and the IRGC cast doubt on Trump&rsquo;s claims the same day. Araghchi&rsquo;s June&nbsp;13 statement stands as the most authoritative forward-leaning framing from the Iranian side, but the formal June&nbsp;1 suspension of negotiations &mdash; issued through state media &mdash; had not been officially and explicitly rescinded in any IRNA or Tasnim dispatch as of 0600 CET June&nbsp;13. Qatar negotiators held sessions in Tehran through the early hours of June&nbsp;11. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
      <p>The Israel-Iran military front entered a fourth consecutive day without a direct exchange as of June&nbsp;13, with the post-June&nbsp;8 pause sustained by commitments extracted by Trump: Israel halted strikes on Iran; Iran declared a suspension of operations against Israel. Both conditions were contingent on restraint &mdash; Israel stated its Lebanon campaign would continue &ldquo;at full force,&rdquo; and Iran issued a warning of &ldquo;more severe&rdquo; retaliation if Lebanon strikes continued. Al Jazeera documented the ceasefire as holding on War Day 102 (June&nbsp;9). Netanyahu stated publicly on June&nbsp;11 that Israel is not a party to the emerging Iran-US deal. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
      <p>Strait of Hormuz transit figures remained at or near historic lows as of June&nbsp;11-13. Iran&rsquo;s IRGC declared the strait &ldquo;closed to all vessels&rdquo; on June&nbsp;11; CENTCOM contested this characterization, noting that some transits occurred. PortWatch data through June&nbsp;7 recorded 2 commercial transits against a 94/day pre-crisis baseline. US mine-clearance operations using the USS Frank E. Peterson and USS Michael Murphy, which began in April 2026, are ongoing. G7 partners with an expanded demining coalition are operationally ready but are awaiting US presidential approval, with the June&nbsp;15-17 G7 summit the next formal decision point. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span></p>
      <p>Israel&rsquo;s domestic political structure continued to fracture through the week of June&nbsp;9-13. The Knesset dissolution bill passed its first reading 106-0 on approximately June&nbsp;1, with coalition whip Katz declining to insert an election date pending committee negotiations. The Haredi draft crisis deepened: i24NEWS documented that the coalition temporarily lost its functioning majority, and Rabbi Dov Lando of Degel HaTorah publicly denounced Netanyahu as a &ldquo;liar&rdquo; and moved his faction toward supporting dissolution. One report referenced an emerging informal agreement on an October&nbsp;20 election date, unconfirmed. The second and third readings of the dissolution bill remain unscheduled. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, supports <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

