      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (35%):</strong> Military exchange probability has fallen materially (<span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F3</span> at 0.35) with a 4-day ceasefire holding and deal momentum active, but Lebanon operations, Iran&rsquo;s conditional threat posture, and Netanyahu&rsquo;s declared non-party status preserve a residual escalation pathway.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (40%):</strong> The most probable single scenario: deal-track is moving but has not closed, Hormuz corridor remains at near-zero commercial transits (<span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F4</span> at 0.20), and the gap between Pakistan PM&rsquo;s announcement and formal IRNA confirmation leaves the diplomatic outcome unresolved.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (25%):</strong> A signing of the MOU this weekend (<span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">NF1</span> at 0.40) combined with a structured session before June&nbsp;24 (<span style="font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F2</span> at 0.62) would constitute the most concrete de-escalation pathway since the conflict began, lifting de-esc from 10% to 25% on the strength of the current diplomatic signal cluster.</p>
